{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '490,610p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md\nrg -n 'defn (index-datoms|ast->datoms)|first-user-id|host|ownership' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc src/cljc/dao/datom.cljc\ncat docs/agents/build-n-test.md .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md\"","aggregated_output":"**resume** ($val$ = resume value, $I.id$ the parked id): the parked\nconfiguration is restored with $val$ as its accumulator.\n**current-continuation**: $val \\leftarrow \\{seg, pc{+}1, E, St, K\\}$\nreified as data.\n\n### 4.3 What the equations make visible\n\nCompare the walker's evaluation of one operand: it allocates a frame map,\n`assoc`s the frame into $k$, `conj`s onto `:evaluated`, and dispatches on a\nkeyword `:type` twice (node, then continuation). The linear machine does\n`push` (one vector `conj`) and increments `pc`. The \"continuation\" of an\noperand sequence is the *next pc*, a fact the lowering already computed.\nThis is the entire performance argument, stated as semantics rather than as\nengineering (§6).\n\n---\n\n## §5. Lowering: Universal AST → linear datoms\n\n### 5.1 Placement and contract\n\n`yin.vm.linearize` (not `yang.linearize`): yang owns syntax → AST and must\nstay ignorant of any evaluator; lowering AST → executable code is VM lineage\nand must stay ignorant of any surface syntax. Clojure, Python and PHP front\nends all reach the linearizer through the one canonical form. The\nrecommendation departs from the target-file example in the task brief for\nthis reason.\n\n```\nlower : [:yin/* datoms] → [:yin.code/* datoms]         ; pure, total over supported node types\nlower-ast : ast-map → [:yin.code/* datoms]             ; convenience = lower ∘ ast->datoms\n```\n\nInput is canonical AST datoms because that is what travels and what the\nwalker consumes; the implementation may reconstruct maps with\n`vm/datoms->ast` internally in Phase 2 and lower from the datom index\ndirectly later. Output is one segment per program root.\n\n### 5.2 When: ahead-of-time at the load boundary\n\nCompilation is AOT per segment, once, where the program crosses into the\nVM: `eval` lowers its AST; a composition whose program stream carries AST\ndatoms composes `lower` into the loader it hands the observer; a composition\nwhose stream carries code datoms hands the loader alone. There is no\ntiering, no recompile-at-boundary, no dirty flag: the engine's versioned\ncompile cache exists for programs that grow by append, and a segment is\nimmutable once emitted. Streaming delivery of a growing segment (a pc past\nthe loaded length parks on code rather than erroring) is reserved for a\nlater phase; it is additive because a pc beyond `length` is already a\ndefined load-time condition.\n\n### 5.3 The flattening\n\nRecursive descent over the AST in evaluation order, emitting instructions\ninto a growing vector and lambda bodies out of line:\n\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| AST node                                                | Emitted sequence                                                   |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:literal v`                                            | `const v`                                                          |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:variable s`                                           | `var s`                                                            |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:lambda ps body`                                       | `closure ps →L` ; body emitted later at label L ending in `return` |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:application op args` (tail? τ)                        | `⟦op⟧ push ⟦a₁⟧ push … ⟦aₙ⟧ push call n τ`                         |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:if t c a`                                             | `⟦t⟧ branch-false →A ⟦c⟧ jump →E A: ⟦a⟧ E:`                        |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:dao.stream.apply/call op args`                        | `⟦a₁⟧ push … ⟦aₙ⟧ push ffi-call op n`                              |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:stream/put target val`                                | `⟦target⟧ push ⟦val⟧ stream-put`                                   |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:stream/cursor s`, `:stream/next s`, `:stream/close s` | `⟦s⟧ stream-cursor` etc.                                           |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:stream/make b`                                        | `stream-make b`                                                    |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:vm/store-get k`, `:vm/store-put k v`, `:vm/gensym p`  | one instruction each                                               |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:vm/park`, `:vm/current-continuation`                  | one instruction each                                               |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:vm/resume id v`                                       | `⟦v⟧ resume id`                                                    |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| root                                                    | main sequence ends in `halt`; bodies follow                        |\n+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nEvaluation order is the walker's (operator, then operands left to right),\nso effect order is identical, which the parity suite checks. Tail position\nis read from `:yin/tail?` as yang already marks it; `:if` branches inherit\nit; nothing new is inferred. Labels are resolved to instruction refs in a\nsecond pass; the linearizer emits refs, never pc integers.\n\nEvery instruction gets `:yin.code/source` = the AST node eid it came from,\nand the segment gets `:yin.code/derived-from` = the root eid. The reverse\ndirection (render an instruction range back to source) is a query, not a\nfunction in this namespace.\n\n---\n\n## §6. Performance architecture\n\n### 6.1 Traversal mechanics\n\nWalker, per AST node: read `:type` (keyword map lookup), destructure the\nnode (2–3 lookups), allocate a continuation frame, and later read the frame\nback (`:frame`, `:operands`, `:evaluated`, `:env`, `:next`: 5 lookups) and\ndispatch on `:type` of the frame. Nodes are heap-scattered persistent maps\nreached by pointer from their parent.\n\nLinear machine, per instruction: `aget` on one array, one integer `case`,\none operation on locals. Operands are fields of the decoded instruction\n(array slots or record fields), read once. The next instruction is the next\narray slot.\n\n### 6.2 Memory and allocation\n\n+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| Event                    | Walker allocations                         | Linear allocations                                     |\n+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| evaluate an operand      | frame map, `assoc k`, `conj evaluated` (3) | `conj` onto `St` (1)                                   |\n+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\nsrc/cljc/dao/datom.cljc:5:   mirror Datomic's `added` boolean and extend it; ids at `first-user-id` and\nsrc/cljc/dao/datom.cljc:27:(def first-user-id\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:8:   - **The host supplies streams.** v1 hardcoded a ring buffer descriptor and\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:129:   ;; Module loading primitive - returns an effect dispatched to the host\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:174:  \"Create one stream through a host-supplied constructor, or fail.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:251:           ;; Keep a local literal map so CLJD macro host compilation\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:252:           ;; does not depend on resolving `opcode-table` as a host\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:372:(defn ast->datoms-with-root\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:385:     :id-start - starting entity ID for tempids (default (- datom/first-user-id))\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:388:   (let [id-counter (atom (or (:id-start opts) (- datom/first-user-id)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:485:(defn ast->datoms\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:565:(defn index-datoms\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:650:   cursor, and gap count belong to `dao.stream.observer`, which a host\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:671:           ;; the error propagates, so an unreachable host resource is not\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # Run all tests (JVM, Node/CLJS, Dart/CLJD)\nbb test:clj          # JVM tests only (or: clj -M:test)\nbb test:cljs         # ClojureScript / Node tests (via shadow-cljs :cljs alias)\nbb test:cljd         # ClojureDart tests (requires flutter on PATH)\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
