{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"head -n 65 src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc\nrg -n 'host|fn\\\\?|ifn\\\\?|serializ|plain.data|literal' test/yin/vm/code_test.cljc docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md\nrg -n 'tail\\\\?|:if' src/cljc/yang -g '*.cljc' | head -n 55\"","aggregated_output":"(ns yin.vm\n  \"Canonical data model (Datoms), schema, and primitives for the Yin Abstract\n   Machine on DaoStream v2.\n\n   The shared kernel of `yin.vm` carries over unchanged. What changes is the\n   stream wiring:\n\n   - **The host supplies streams.** v1 hardcoded a ring buffer descriptor and\n     called `ds/open!`. Here a composition hands the interpreter a\n     `:make-stream` constructor exactly as it hands it `:primitives`. There is\n     no default, because a default would smuggle the hardcoded transport back\n     in: absent a supplied constructor, `:stream/make` is unsupported and says\n     so.\n   - **Program observation lives outside the VM.** The program stream handle,\n     cursor, and gap count belong to `dao.stream.observer`; the VM never\n     polls a program stream and no longer accepts `:in-stream` at\n     construction. Language-level stream effects and FFI still operate their\n     own streams and cursors inside the VM.\n   - **Cursors are opaque.** The VM's internal cursor representation is\n     `{:stream-id id :cursor <opaque>}`. There is no position and no seek.\n   - **Construction is all-or-nothing.** Creating the FFI pair and minting its\n     cursors are stream operations with their own outcomes. Any non-`ok`\n     outcome fails construction; a VM cannot be half-built.\n   - **`call-in-cursor-key` is dropped.** v1 wrote it and nothing read it.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [eval])\n  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [yin.vm.telemetry :as telemetry]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Protocols\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Common VM interfaces implemented by all execution engines.\n\n(defprotocol IVM\n  \"Unified VM protocol for execution, evaluation, and observable state.\"\n\n  (step\n    [vm]\n    \"Execute one step of already-loaded work. An idle VM is returned\n     unchanged; program input arrives through an attached stream observer.\")\n\n  (run\n    [vm]\n    \"Run already-loaded work until halted or blocked.\")\n\n  (eval\n    [vm ast]\n    \"Convert and evaluate a supplied AST. Does not drain any independently\n     queued program input; explicit observer coordination does that.\")\n\n  (reset\n    [vm]\n    \"Reset execution state to a known initial state, preserving loaded program.\")\n\n  (halted?\n    [vm]\n    \"Returns true if VM has halted (completed or error).\")\n\n  (blocked?\n    [vm]\n    \"Returns true if VM is blocked waiting for external input.\")\n\n  (value\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:24:one consumer, one order, no replay need, and no host boundary; putting a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:37:Execution state is plain data: the control is `{:segment id :pc n}`, the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:45:`:parked`; resume reads it back. Nothing in that map is a host object except\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:46:values the program itself put in `env` or on the stack (host functions\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:48:serialization limit the walker has today. A continuation shipped through a\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:49:stream names its segment by id; the receiving host resolves the id against\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:208:`:literal :load-var :lambda :call :return :branch :jump :gensym :store-get\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:211:maps mnemonics onto that table (`:const`→`:literal`, `:var`→`:load-var`,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:218:### 2.5 Constants and literals\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:220:A literal lives inline in the `v` slot of `:yin.code/value`, polymorphic\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:224:`:primitives`, which is how the walker already avoids putting host objects in\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:288:- The host composes a program medium, attaches `yin.vm.stream-observer`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:331:that never ran; the REPL latch stays host policy); `cursor-mismatch`,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:363:call naming the outcome. The host side (`ffi/bridge-step`, `ffi/maybe-run`)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:549:| `:literal v`                                            | `const v`                                                          |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:637:benchmarks put semantic bytecode at 2–7x slower on those hosts precisely\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:670:Because execution state is modeled as pure data rather than host memory (Axiom 4), the linear bytecode state serves as the universal *lingua franca* for network-transparent continuations. The lowered representation (`{:segment id, :pc n, :env E, :stack S, :k K}`) resolves all execution-order ambiguity inherent in the Universal AST while remaining entirely decoupled from any specific hardware architecture.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:674:- **On Park (De-optimization):** When the specialized VM hits a blocking IO operation or a `:vm/park` instruction, it must map its specialized hardware registers or host stack back to the normalized linear format using the `:yin.code/source` mappings. It emits this standard Semantic VM continuation onto the stream.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:686:To safely \"lift on park\" and \"lower on resume\" across heterogeneous boundaries without violating host isolation or concurrency invariants, the following contracts must be fully specified before this feature is accepted:\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:689:2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive serialization protocol is required to encode these local references into portable descriptors, with explicit failure modes for un-serializable resources.\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:123:  [fn-name params body-forms env tail? macro-env]\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:132:      tail? (assoc :tail? true))))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:145:  [form _tail?]\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:151:  [sym _tail?]\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:158:  ([sym value env tail?] (compile-def sym value env tail? initial-macro-env))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:159:  ([sym value env tail? macro-env]\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:164:     tail? (assoc :tail? true))))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:174:  ([params body env tail?] (compile-lambda params body env tail? initial-macro-env))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:175:  ([params body env tail? macro-env]\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:179:       tail? (assoc :tail? true)))))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:189:  ([operator operands env tail?] (compile-application operator operands env tail? initial-macro-env))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:190:  ([operator operands env tail? macro-env]\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:196:       tail? (assoc :tail? true)))))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:206:  ([op operands env tail?] (compile-dao-stream-apply-call op operands env tail? initial-macro-env))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:207:  ([op operands env tail? macro-env]\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:214:       tail? (assoc :tail? true)))))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:221:  AST: {:type :if\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:225:  ([test consequent alternate env tail?] (compile-if test consequent alternate env tail? initial-macro-env))\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:226:  ([test consequent alternate env tail? macro-env]\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:227:   (cond-> {:type :if,\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:229:            :consequent (compile-form consequent tail? env macro-env),\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:231:                         (compile-form alternate tail? env macro-env)\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:232:                         (compile-literal nil 